  "summary": "Jansi has a heap buffer overflow in its JNI ioctl() wrapper",
  "details": "A heap buffer overflow exists in the native (JNI) implementation of `org.fusesource.jansi.internal.CLibrary.ioctl(int, long, int[])`. The wrapper passes the Java array's elements straight to `ioctl()` without checking that the array is large enough for the request. The kernel writes as many bytes as the request defines (for example, `TCGETS` writes a 36-byte `struct termios` on Linux x86-64), so a caller that passes a short `int[]` corrupts the native heap and can crash the JVM (denial of service).\n\nAll versions through 2.4.3 are affected. 2.4.3 is the last release: the project was deprecated in March 2026 and is unmaintained, so no fixed version exists.",
  "severity": [
    {
      "type": "CVSS_V4",
      "score": "CVSS:4.0/AV:L/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:P/VC:N/VI:N/VA:L/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N"
    }
  ],
